---
import CaseStudyPage from "@/components/CaseStudyPage.astro";
import "@/styles/components/case-study.css";

const description =
  "Llamail is a self-hosted email agent with deterministic routing, local hybrid retrieval, cited answers, and human-reviewed actions through Telegram.";
---

<CaseStudyPage
  slug="llamail"
  title="Llamail"
  description={description}
  kickerLabel="SYSTEM"
  eyebrow="Independent local-first email agent · 2026"
  status="SELF-HOSTED · NO PUBLIC DEMO"
  lead="A private email system designed to let a small local model search, explain, draft, and route useful actions without treating the model as unrestricted application logic."
  ownership="Designed and implemented end to end: system architecture, FastAPI services, routing, retrieval, local-model integration, storage, Telegram workflow, and operator experience."
  primaryAction={{
    label: "Read the implementation series →",
    href: "/articles/private-local-ai-email-agent",
    external: false,
  }}
  overviewTitle="Deterministic application behavior, with the model used where language helps."
  evidenceTitle="A real interface and the system behind it."
  evidenceIntro="The Telegram capture is sanitized portfolio evidence. The architecture diagram shows which work stays local and where external services remain part of the route."
  boundaryTitle="Local-first does not mean every dependency is local."
  facts={[
    { label: "Role", value: "Independent system · implemented end to end" },
    { label: "Runtime", value: "FastAPI · llama.cpp · local GPU" },
    { label: "Retrieval", value: "ChromaDB + SQLite FTS5" },
    { label: "Interface", value: "Telegram via n8n" },
  ]}
  overview={[
    "Llamail keeps predictable commands on explicit handlers. Slash commands and recognized compound commands route directly; free-form requests use the local model as a conservative intent classifier, then normal application code validates and executes the selected handler.",
    "Sable is the Telegram interface and operator persona for Llamail, not a separate email platform. It presents cited answers, reports incoming-email analysis, and keeps consequential actions visible for human review instead of giving the model direct, unbounded control.",
  ]}
  highlights={[
    {
      title: "Deterministic routing first",
      description:
        "Known commands avoid model latency and ambiguity. Natural-language classification is a fallback that returns structured intent for validated application handlers.",
    },
    {
      title: "Hybrid retrieval with citations",
      description:
        "Semantic search in ChromaDB and keyword search in SQLite FTS5 are combined before generation, so answers can point back to the retrieved email evidence.",
    },
    {
      title: "Human-reviewed actions",
      description:
        "Drafting and mailbox actions remain explicit operator workflows. The interface shows what the system understood before a consequential action is accepted.",
    },
    {
      title: "Measured on local hardware",
      description:
        "Personal measurements on an RTX 4080 Mobile 12 GB system were about ~3 seconds for hybrid search, ~7 seconds for retrieval plus answer generation, and ~4 seconds for natural-language intent classification. These are hardware-specific observations, not a performance guarantee.",
    },
  ]}
  relatedArticles={[
    {
      title: "From Inbox to Character: Creating a Private, Local AI Email Agent",
      summary: "The complete system, local-model runtime, Telegram interface, storage, and operating boundary.",
      href: "/articles/private-local-ai-email-agent",
    },
    {
      title: "How /search and /ask Work: Local Hybrid RAG with ChromaDB + SQLite FTS5",
      summary: "Weighted semantic and keyword retrieval across a large mailbox, with relevance-aware citations.",
      href: "/articles/local-hybrid-rag-chromadb-sqlite-fts5",
    },
    {
      title: "LLM as Router: Intent Classification for a Local Telegram Email Agent",
      summary: "Slash commands and deterministic handlers first; natural-language classification only as a fallback.",
      href: "/articles/llm-as-router-intent-classification",
    },
    {
      title: "Implementation Lessons: Hidden Headaches of a Local Gmail AI Agent",
      summary: "Gmail synchronization, labels, threads, identifiers, and the failure modes that shaped the design.",
      href: "/articles/hidden-headaches-local-gmail-ai-agent",
    },
    {
      title: "LLM-Personalized Email Campaigns with Python, Gmail, and Local AI",
      summary: "Previewed personalization, throttled delivery, reply tracking, and conservative classification.",
      href: "/articles/llm-personalized-email-campaigns",
    },
  ]}
  evidence={[
    {
      src: "/work/llamail-telegram.jpg",
      alt: "Sanitized Telegram capture of Sable returning a cited Llamail answer and an incoming-email analysis report",
      width: 1080,
      height: 2340,
      caption: "Sanitized Sable Telegram UI · cited answer and operator-visible email analysis",
      format: "mobile",
    },
    {
      src: "/work/llamail/llamail-architecture.png",
      alt: "Llamail architecture diagram separating Telegram and Gmail services from the local FastAPI, retrieval, storage, and model runtime",
      width: 840,
      height: 418,
      caption: "System boundary · external Gmail and Telegram routes around local inference, retrieval, and state",
    },
  ]}
  boundaries={[
    "Local inference, embeddings, application services, SQLite FTS5, and ChromaDB run on the owner’s machine.",
    "Gmail and Telegram remain external services. Gmail API access and Telegram messages cross those service boundaries, so this is not a zero-cloud claim.",
    "Llamail is self-hosted software rather than a hosted product. There is no public live demo or public Telegram handle because operation requires user-owned integrations and local model hardware.",
    "The measurements above came from personal hardware and real development use. They do not claim universal latency, adoption, or production-scale qualification.",
  ]}
/>
